  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY NIGHT THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 219 AM CDT Fri Sep 18 2026\n\nSaturday Night-Sunday...After sunset, a developing high \npressure over central Canada nudges the surface trough \nsoutheastward into Illinois Saturday night. Despite the low-\nlevel CAA, another band of moderate-to-heavy rainfall will \nlikely (70-90%) develop and persist into Sunday. While there is\nstill uncertainty among the ensemble models on placement of the\nheaviest axis, general consensus is showing rainfall totals of \n0.25- 0.75 of an inch (50-80% chance) across a broad area with a\nswath of 1-2 inches possible (30-50%). The coverage and \nintensity of these showers should begin to decrease by Sunday \nafternoon as dry air advection occurs near the surface.\n\nSunday Night-Tuesday...The main trough over the Northern \nRockies finally reaches the Northern Plains as low-to-mid-level \nridging over south-central Canada continues to push the surface\ntrough further south into the lower Ohio River Valley. Despite\nthe cold air advection, CVA ahead of the trough keeps scattered\nlight-to-moderate showers (50-70% coverage) around Sunday night\ninto Monday. By Monday night, the trough finally reaches Iowa \nas cold-core showers form underneath, keeping PoPs around \nthrough Tuesday morning. As subsidence sets in on the backside \nof the trough and high pressure builds in from the north, the \noutlook area should be rain-free by Tuesday evening.\n\nWednesday-Thursday...Fall-like weather is likely (70-90%) to \nreturn for the middle of next week. Confluence aloft and surface\nhigh pressure is forecasted to keep Wednesday and Thursday dry \nas CAA keeps temperatures below average with highs in the \nmid-60s to low 70s and lows in the mid-40s to low 50s.",
